      "text": "The x-intercept \\((a,0)\\) occurs when \\(x = 0\\). Therefore, we solve for \\(y\\) in the equation \\[\n2y^2 - 6y + 3 = 0.\n\\] We can use the quadratic formula: \\begin{align*}\n2y^2 - 6y + 3 &= 0, \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-(-6) \\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^2 - 4(2)(3)}}{2(2)} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 24}}{4} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{12}}{4} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n\\blacksquare y &= \\frac{3}{2} \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\end{align*}Therefore , \\[\na = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n\\] and \\[\nb = c = \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\] We have \\[\na + b + c = 2 \\frac{3}{2} = \\boxed{3}.\n\\] President2\n\n\nProblem Set 4 Solve one of the following tasks in the \\emph{Math Task Book}. (Solve all four problems to earn extra credit.)\nBefore doing so, spend some time thinking about the problem. What strategies can you think of for solving it? Brainstorming, pretending to be someone else, considering extreme situations, taking logical leaps, etc, are all fruitful if they are creative and result in nontrivial solutions.\n\nExecution:\nLet \\(f\\) be a nonzero, real-valued function defined on all the real numbers that satisfies the equation$\n$f(x + f(y)) = f(x)+y$.\nFind the number of possible values of \\(f(0)\\).\n\nSolution:\nNote that $f(-a) = \\text{undefined}$ if $a = \\text{undefined}$, i.e $f(0) = -\\infty$, and $\\text{undefined}$. If $y = \\infty$, or $- \\infty$, it’s fine. Now, let $a = \\infty$,$y = 0$, equation becomes $f(\\infty) = f(\\infty) + 0$, which isn’t possible.\n\nObviously $f(-\\infty)$ can’t work as intended.\n\nWe know that $f(-f(0)) = -f(0)$. Putting $x = 0,-f(0)$, we see that then $$\\begin{align*}\nf(-f(0) + f(-f(-f(0))) & =f(-f(0)) - f(0), \\\\\nf(-f(0) - f(-f(0))) &= f(-f(0)) - f(0).\n\\end{align*}$$Thus, an obvious observation is $$\\boxed{ f(0) \\in (-1,0) \\cup (0,1)}.$$\nHence no other possible value, it’s satisfied for all the values. Therefore, all these $2$ possibilities. $\\blacksquare$\nPresident2\n\nIntermediate Value Theorem: there is a value in $x \\in k \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $ f(x)=k$. But for $\\lim \\limits_{x \\to k} f(x)=\\text{undefined}$ for all $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$. That tells us that we have an undefined y-coordiante at $f(0)$.\n+-\nBy substituting $y \\to -\\infty$, we get $f(-f(0) + (-\\infty)) =(-\\infty)-f(0)$   certainly impossible, same argument with $\\infty$.\n\nAfter doing my 4/8 math for marathion MATH MASTER OF THE GLORY  (Green Only EN  :clap: )\n\nCiao",
